/-!
# The direct image of the sections annihilated by an ideal sheaf

Let `f : X ⟶ Y` be a morphism of algebraic spaces over a scheme `S` contained in a big fppf
site `B`, let `I ⊆ 𝒪_Y` be a sheaf of ideals and let `M` be an `𝒪_X`-module.  Writing
`f^{-1}I · 𝒪_X ⊆ 𝒪_X` for the sheaf of ideals generated by the pullbacks of the sections of
`I`, [Stacks, Tag 0858] asserts that the direct image of the subsheaf of sections of `M`
annihilated by `f^{-1}I · 𝒪_X` is the subsheaf of sections of `f_*M` annihilated by `I`.  The
two subsheaves are the ones named by [Stacks, Tag 0857].

The direct image is taken on the spaces-étale sites of [Stacks, Tag 03G0], where it is
restriction along the cartesian base-change functor of [Stacks, Tag 03G2] with scalars
restricted along the structure map of [Stacks, Tag 03G8] — the direct image of modules on a
morphism of ringed sites of [Stacks, Tag 03CM].  Accordingly the lemma is proved for an
arbitrary morphism of ringed sites and then specialised.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.Sites.PairwiseCofiltered` is the condition on a functor of sites that any two
  morphisms of the target into images of objects of the source factor through the image of a
  single object.
* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.inverseImageIdeal` is `G^{-1}I · R'` for a morphism of ringed sites.
* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.pushforwardSubmodule` is the direct image `G_*N ⊆ G_*M` of a submodule
  sheaf `N ⊆ M`.
* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.restrictionAnnihilatorIdeal` is the ideal subsheaf of `R'` annihilating
  all restrictions of one section of `M` coming from the base site.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.spacesEtaleSharpRing` is the structure map of [Stacks, Tag 03G8] on
  the spaces-étale sites, as a map of sheaves of rings.

## Main results

* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.mem_annihilatorSubmodule_pushforward_iff` identifies, section by
  section, the annihilator of `I` in `G_*M` with the annihilator of `G^{-1}I · R'` in `M`.
* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.pushforwardSubmodule_annihilatorSubmodule` is the same identification
  as an equality of submodule sheaves of `G_*M`, for an arbitrary morphism of ringed sites.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.pairwiseCofiltered_spacesBaseChange`: spaces-étale base change is
  pairwise cofiltered.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.pushforwardSubmodule_annihilatorSubmodule` is
  **[Stacks, Tag 0858]** for a morphism of algebraic spaces.

## Implementation notes

All three hypotheses of the source are discharged as unnecessary rather than assumed.
Quasi-coherence and finite type of `I`, and quasi-coherence of `M`, are needed in the source
only so that the subsheaf of [Stacks, Tag 0857] is available, and quasi-compactness and
quasi-separatedness of `f` only so that `f_*M` is quasi-coherent (Stacks 03M9, which this
library does not have); the construction `Stacks.SheafOfModules.annihilatorSubmodule` asks for
none of them, so none is used and nothing is weakened.

The direct image is the spaces-étale one and not
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.modulePushforward`, which is the same functor read through the
comparison equivalence of [Stacks, Tag 03G1] on the *small* étale sites.  That functor is
built by conjugating the spaces-étale direct image with a chosen quasi-inverse of the
comparison, so it is not a restriction along a functor of small étale sites and its sections
are not the sections of `M` over a base change; the library does not identify it with one, and
the obstruction is recorded in the implementation notes of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/SmallEtaleBaseChangeRestriction.lean` and
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/ModulePushforwardChartRestriction.lean`.  For the same
reason the subsheaf appearing here is the general-site construction
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.annihilatorSubmodule` read on the spaces-étale ringed site, rather than
its small-étale reading `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.annihilatorSubmodule`.

`G^{-1}I · R'` is the infimum of the ideal subsheaves of `R'` whose sections over `G U` contain
the pullbacks of the sections of `I` over `U`; this is the house form for an ideal sheaf
generated by prescribed sections, as in `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.fittingIdealPullback`.  Only
two properties of it are used: that it contains those pullbacks and that it is the smallest
such.

`Stacks.Sites.PairwiseCofiltered` is what the inclusion of the annihilator of `I` in `G_*M`
into the direct image needs.  A section `s` of `M` over `G V` killed by the pullbacks of `I`
must be killed by every section of `G^{-1}I · R'` over every object `W` of the source site
above `G V`, including objects admitting no map compatible with the data at hand: the pullback
of a section of `I` over some unrelated `U` restricts to `W` as well, and the hypothesis at `V`
says nothing about it until `W` is seen to lie above the image of a common refinement of `U`
and `V`.  On the spaces-étale site that refinement is the fibre product over the base, which
exists there, and base change preserves it and the terminal object
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.preservesPullbacks_spacesBaseChange` and
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isTerminal_spacesBaseChange_idObj`), so the condition holds for every
morphism of algebraic spaces and is not an extra assumption.

`CoverPreserving` enters only through `Stacks.SheafOfModules.pushforwardSubmodule`: the direct
image of a submodule sheaf is a submodule sheaf because a covering sieve of `V` pushes forward
to a covering sieve of `G V`.  For a continuous functor it is automatic.

Commutativity of the two rings of sections is carried as a hypothesis rather than as an
instance, in the shape `Stacks.SheafOfModules.annihilatorSubmodule` consumes; on a ringed site
it is `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.mul_comm_ringSheaf`.

